<h2>Indian chickpeas for export</h2>
<p>India is the world's largest chickpea producer, and our 2024 export data shows a rare thing for an Indian pulse: a genuinely diversified buyer base rather than dependence on one or two dominant markets. We supply both Kabuli (the large, cream-coloured type used whole) and Desi (smaller, darker, typically split into chana dal or milled into besan), cleaned and sized to your specification.</p>

<p>We source from Madhya Pradesh — India's Kabuli heartland — and Maharashtra, with Rajasthan, Karnataka and Andhra Pradesh adding volume, mostly of Desi-type. Stock is sized, cleaned to your foreign-matter and admixture tolerance, and bagged or jumbo-bagged for container loading.</p>

<h2>Chickpea export policy: settled, not volatile</h2>
<p>Worth stating plainly because pulses as a category have a complicated Indian export history: several pulses, chickpeas included, were prohibited from export for years to protect domestic supply and prices. DGFT lifted that prohibition in September 2017 "till further orders," and it has remained in force since. Kabuli chana specifically had its own standalone exemption dating back to March 2007, even before the broader 2017 lift.</p>
<p>Unlike fresh onion — where we caveat every quote because the Minimum Export Price regime genuinely changes at short notice — chickpea export policy has been stable for years now. Real 2024 trade flows (see the table below) confirm this isn't just a policy statement on paper; substantial volume is actually moving. We still confirm current terms at time of order as standard practice across every product, but this is not a line where we expect policy surprises.</p>

<h2>How should I specify chickpeas?</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Type.</strong> Kabuli or Desi — they serve different end uses and price differently. Say which you need.</li>
  <li><strong>Size (Kabuli).</strong> Graded by count-per-ounce — 7mm, 8mm, 9mm and 10mm+ are common export bands, with larger sizes commanding a premium.</li>
  <li><strong>Moisture and cleanliness.</strong> 12–14% moisture and low foreign matter/admixture are standard export tolerances — tell us your contract limits.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Which markets buy Indian chickpeas?</h2>
<p>The UAE and Bangladesh are the two largest by value, with Iran, Algeria and Sri Lanka close behind — no single market dominates the way Bangladesh dominates, say, Indian lentil exports. That spread is a genuine advantage if you're evaluating supply-risk diversification against India as an origin.</p>

